{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0162dx2Kjo8EtSzEjg52XUM4","type":"tool_result","content":"     1\t\"\"\"Complete search over customer closing orders (Chu & Stuckey 2009, §2-3).\n     2\t\n     3\tAn alternative to the SAT encoding for deciding \"MOSP(I) <= k?\". It searches the\n     4\tspace the plan's §0.1 established is the right one -- orders in which customers'\n     5\tstacks *close* -- rather than the space of product sequences, and prunes it with\n     6\tthe dominance relations of their §3, none of which can be written as clauses\n     7\tbecause every one conditions on the partial sequence already committed.\n     8\t\n     9\tWhy this exists alongside the SAT path: their Table 1 reports the complete\n    10\tsearch closing 125-125-6, 125-125-8 and 125-125-10 in 9 s, 0.19 s and 0.02 s,\n    11\tagainst our SAT refutations, which do not return on those instances at all.\n    12\tThe densities where their search is fastest are the ones holding most of our\n    13\topen gap. It is also the reverse of the SAT path's profile -- their search is\n    14\tslowest exactly where sparsity makes the branching factor explode, which is what\n    15\ttheir relaxation technique (plan item 5) exists to repair.\n    16\t\n    17\t**The measure.** A state is the set `S` of customers already closed. `O(S)` is\n    18\tthe set opened so far, the union of the neighbourhoods `N(c)` over `c ∈ S`, with\n    19\t`N` self-inclusive. Closing `c` next costs `|O(S ∪ {c}) - S|`: the stacks opened\n    20\tbut not yet closed, counting `c` itself, which is still open at the moment it\n    21\tcloses. `min` over closing orders of the peak of this equals the true optimum --\n    22\tmeasured 400/400 against exhaustive search, `reports/chu_stuckey_plan.md` §0.1.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Free moves.** A remaining customer whose whole neighbourhood is already opened\n    25\tcosts nothing to close and is closed immediately. This is the `open = 0` case of\n    26\ttheir Theorem 1 and it is what keeps the measure above honest: without it, a\n    27\tcustomer whose products are all made would keep counting as an open stack, and\n    28\tthe \"close\" counts the dominance rules below are stated in terms of would be\n    29\twrong.\n    30\t\n    31\t**The rules.** Each is a dominance: it discards branches that cannot beat the\n    32\tbranches kept, so a refutation still refutes.\n    33\t\n    34\t- *subset* (their §2): if `o(cᵢ,S) ⊆ o(cⱼ,S)` then closing `cᵢ` first is never\n    35\t  worse, since closing `cⱼ` opens everything `cᵢ` would have and closing `cᵢ`\n    36\t  can only help `cⱼ` -- so `cⱼ` leaves the candidate set.\n    37\t- *definite move* (their Theorem 1): if `close(q,S) ≥ open(q,S)` and `S ++ [q]`\n    38\t  is playable, then every solution extending `S` has one extending `S ++ [q]`,\n    39\t  so **all** other branches go.\n    40\t- *old move* (their Theorem 3): if `q` was already searched at an ancestor and\n    41\t  inserting `q` back there leaves the sequence playable, that subtree has been\n    42\t  seen and `q` goes. Maintained as a set `Q(S)` in `O(|C|)` per node.\n    43\t- *better move* (their Theorem 2): if `S ++ [q]` and `S ++ [r, q]` are playable\n    44\t  and `close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r})`, then `r` goes. `O(|R|³)` per node\n    45\t  against Theorem 1's `O(|R|²)`, implemented in the C only, and **worth it only\n    46\t  on sparse instances** -- see `sparse_enough_for_better_move`.\n    47\t\n    48\t**Old move and the memo, together.** Chu & Stuckey run both -- \"better move\",\n    49\t\"old move\" and nogood recording on at once -- and the C follows them. The\n    50\tPython still refuses the combination, because the argument against it has not\n    51\tbeen retired: a failure reached with old-move pruning depends on which branches\n    52\tan *ancestor* had searched, so it is a property of the path rather than of `S`,\n    53\tand recording it against `S` alone could refute a state that some other path\n    54\twould not. Whether that can actually happen is settled by the exhaustive tests\n    55\tin `tests/test_native.py`, not by the argument.\n    56\t\"\"\"\n    57\t\n    58\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n    59\t\n    60\timport time\n    61\tfrom collections.abc import Callable\n    62\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n    63\t\n    64\tfrom mosp.instance import MOSPInstance\n    65\tfrom mosp.verify import max_open_stacks\n    66\tfrom satisfiability.heuristics import (\n    67\t    FAN_ORDERS,\n    68\t    _neighbour_masks,\n    69\t    fan_sort_key,\n    70\t    product_order_from_customers,\n    71\t)\n    72\t\n    73\t\n    74\t@dataclass\n    75\tclass Decision:\n    76\t    \"\"\"The answer to \"MOSP(I) <= k?\", and what it cost to get it.\n    77\t\n    78\t    `status` is \"sat\" with a closing order, \"unsat\" -- a genuine refutation, the\n    79\t    whole space having been searched -- or \"unknown\", which means the node\n    80\t    budget ran out and nothing was proved either way. A restricted search never\n    81\t    returns \"unsat\": the restriction discards branches it cannot justify, so\n    82\t    exhausting what remains proves nothing.\n    83\t    \"\"\"\n    84\t\n    85\t    status: str\n    86\t    order: list[int] | None\n    87\t    nodes: int\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\t# Products per customer below which Theorem 2 pays. Measured on refutations\n    91\t# whose answers were already known, counting branch decisions, which is the one\n    92\t# figure a loaded machine cannot distort:\n    93\t#\n    94\t#   products/customer   nodes without   with Theorem 2   verdict\n    95\t#   2.6 (open instance)   263,000,000+        885,759    closed it, in 3s\n    96\t#   2.3                     7,327,112         55,424     15x faster\n    97\t#   6.1                    13,300,731      7,825,793     0.8x, a slight loss\n    98\t#   8.0                    11,902,214      8,652,247     0.45x, a clear loss\n    99\t#\n   100\t# The rule inverts with density because the theorem ranges over pairs of\n   101\t# candidates: sparse instances keep many candidates playable at each step, which\n   102\t# is exactly where pruning a pair at a time repays its cubic cost. Dense ones\n   103\t# run out of candidates quickly and the cost is wasted.\n   104\tBETTER_MOVE_DENSITY = 5.0\n   105\t\n   106\t\n   107\tdef sparse_enough_for_better_move(instance: MOSPInstance) -> bool:\n   108\t    \"\"\"Whether Theorem 2 should be used on this instance.\n   109\t\n   110\t    Chosen by measurement rather than by the instance's name: the Chu & Stuckey\n   111\t    generator's density parameter is not recorded in the file, and the ratio\n   112\t    below reproduces it closely enough (2.57 for a \"-2-\" instance, 8.01 for\n   113\t    a \"-8-\").\n   114\t    \"\"\"\n   115\t    if instance.n_customers == 0:\n   116\t        return False\n   117\t    return float(instance.matrix.sum()) / instance.n_customers <= BETTER_MOVE_DENSITY\n   118\t\n   119\t\n   120\tdef decide(\n   121\t    instance: MOSPInstance,\n   122\t    k: int,\n   123\t    *,\n   124\t    restrict: bool = False,\n   125\t    subset_rule: bool = True,\n   126\t    definite_move: bool = True,\n   127\t    old_move: bool = True,\n   128\t    memo: bool = True,\n   129\t    max_nodes: int | None = None,\n   130\t    deadline: float | None = None,\n   131\t    memo_limit: int = 4_000_000,\n   132\t    native: bool = True,\n   133\t    branch: \"Callable[[int, int, list[tuple[int, int]]], list[tuple[int, int]]] | None\" = None,\n   134\t    expansion_prune: bool = False,\n   135\t    fan_order: str = \"index\",\n   136\t    **kwargs: object,\n   137\t) -> Decision:\n   138\t    \"\"\"Decide \"MOSP(instance) <= k?\" by searching customer closing orders.\n   139\t\n   140\t    Args:\n   141\t        k: the budget on simultaneously open stacks.\n   142\t        restrict: branch only on `R ∩ O(S)`, customers already open. This is\n   143\t            their `ub_MOSP` (§3.4) and is **not sound** -- it answers \"sat\" or\n   144\t            \"unknown\", never \"unsat\".\n   145\t        subset_rule, definite_move, old_move: the dominance relations above. The\n   146\t            flags exist so the exhaustive tests can vary one at a time.\n   147\t            `old_move` cannot be combined with `memo`, for the reason above.\n   148\t        memo: record states already refuted, their `prob[S]`. The state is\n   149\t            `S` alone -- `O(S)` is a function of it -- so a failure at `S` is a\n   150\t            failure by whatever path reached it.\n   151\t        max_nodes: abort after this many branches and return \"unknown\".\n   152\t        deadline: `time.monotonic()` value to stop at, checked every 4,096\n   153\t            nodes. Same effect as `max_nodes`, in the unit a caller with a\n   154\t            budget actually has.\n   155\t        memo_limit: stop growing the memo past this many states. The table is\n   156\t            what bounds memory here, and an unbounded one on a 125-customer\n   157\t            refutation will take the machine down.\n   158\t        native: use the C port when it applies. It visits the same nodes in the\n   159\t            same order -- the counts match exactly on every instance measured --\n   160\t            about 120x faster. Set False to force the Python, which is the\n   161\t            reference implementation and what the exhaustive tests are written\n   162\t            against. `satisfiability/native.py` says when the C declines.\n   163\t        expansion_prune: cut a whole node when even the best continuation must\n   164\t            exceed `k`, by the neighbourhood-expansion argument applied to the\n   165\t            customers still remaining rather than to the instance as a whole\n   166\t            (`reports/expansion_bound.md`). The rule generalises the cost cut\n   167\t            this search already makes: that cut is the one-step case, and every\n   168\t            further step is new pruning. Costs one pass over data the node has\n   169\t            already computed. **Forces the Python path** -- the C does not\n   170\t            implement it -- so it is for measuring whether a port is worth\n   171\t            writing.\n   172\t        branch: reorder the surviving candidates at each node. Called with\n   173\t            `(closed, opened, playable)` where `playable` is the `(cost,\n   174\t            customer)` list the dominance rules left, and must return a\n   175\t            permutation of it -- dropping a candidate would make a refutation\n   176\t            unsound, and the caller is trusted not to. Default is cheapest\n   177\t            first. **Forces the Python path**: the C has no way to call back,\n   178\t            so this is for measuring whether a different order is worth porting,\n   179\t            not for production. Order affects only *which* branches are visited\n   180\t            first: the cost cut, the memo and every dominance rule are\n   181\t            properties of the state, not of the order they are reached in.\n   182\t        fan_order: how equal-cost candidates are ordered. `\"index\"` (the\n   183\t            default, and what every recorded node count was made with) breaks\n   184\t            ties by customer index; `\"degree\"` puts the candidate with the\n   185\t            most neighbours not yet closed first, then index -- the fan order\n   186\t            of the two-key rule in `reports/ml_nature.md` §7. Implemented in\n   187\t            both the C and the Python, so it costs nothing to ask for. Like\n   188\t            `branch`, it changes which branches are visited first and never\n   189\t            which are visited, so the answer is the same under either; the\n   190\t            node count is what it is measured on (plan 2 §2.5a).\n   191\t\n   192\t    Returns:\n   193\t        A `Decision`. The \"sat\" order closes every customer with a non-empty\n   194\t        product set; customers needing nothing are omitted, as they never open.\n   195\t    \"\"\"\n   196\t    if fan_order not in FAN_ORDERS:\n   197\t        raise ValueError(f\"fan_order must be one of {FAN_ORDERS}, not {fan_order!r}\")\n   198\t\n   199\t    if native and branch is None and not expansion_prune:\n   200\t        from satisfiability.native import decide_native\n   201\t        answer = decide_native(\n   202\t            instance, k, restrict=restrict, subset_rule=subset_rule,\n   203\t            definite_move=definite_move, old_move=old_move, memo=memo,\n   204\t            better_move=kwargs.pop(\"better_move\", False),\n   205\t            better_move_dominators=kwargs.pop(\"better_move_dominators\", 4),\n   206\t            # Reverts of the 2026-09-26 fix, unsound, for measurement only\n   207\t            # (learning.fix_cost); meaningless without `better_move`.\n   208\t            old_close_count=bool(kwargs.pop(\"old_close_count\", False)),\n   209\t            old_rule_order=bool(kwargs.pop(\"old_rule_order\", False)),\n   210\t            subset_after_better_move=bool(kwargs.pop(\"subset_after_better_move\", False)),\n   211\t            max_nodes=max_nodes, memo_limit=memo_limit,\n   212\t            seconds=None if deadline is None else max(0.0, deadline - time.monotonic()),\n   213\t            fan_order=fan_order)\n   214\t        if answer is not None:\n   215\t            return answer\n   216\t\n   217\t    if old_move and memo:\n   218\t        # The C runs both, as Chu & Stuckey do, and 10,476 exhaustive decisions\n   219\t        # agree with brute force. This Python reference keeps the stricter\n   220\t        # reading -- the memo is dropped rather than combined -- so that the two\n   221\t        # implementations are not both resting on the same assumption.\n   222\t        memo = False\n   223\t\n   224\t    masks = _neighbour_masks(instance)\n   225\t    active = [c for c in range(instance.n_customers) if instance.customer_patterns(c)]\n   226\t\n   227\t    full = 0\n   228\t    for customer in active:\n   229\t        full |= 1 << customer\n   230\t\n   231\t    if not active:\n   232\t        return Decision(\"sat\", [], 0)\n   233\t\n   234\t    path: list[int] = []\n   235\t    refuted: set[int] = set()\n   236\t    state = {\"nodes\": 0, \"aborted\": False}\n   237\t\n   238\t    def free_moves(closed: int, opened: int) -> int:\n   239\t        \"\"\"Customers whose neighbourhood is wholly opened: free to close now.\n   240\t\n   241\t        Closing one opens nothing, so the peak cannot rise, and it can only\n   242\t        release later steps. Closing them does not change `O`, so no further\n   243\t        free moves appear and one pass suffices.\n   244\t        \"\"\"\n   245\t        found = 0\n   246\t        bits = full & ~closed\n   247\t        while bits:\n   248\t            bit = bits & -bits\n   249\t            bits ^= bit\n   250\t            if masks[bit.bit_length() - 1] & ~opened == 0:\n   251\t                found |= bit\n   252\t        return found\n   253\t\n   254\t    def search(closed: int, opened: int, seen: int = 0) -> bool:\n   255\t        \"\"\"`seen` is Q(S): moves an ancestor already searched that could be\n   256\t        played here instead, without making the sequence unplayable.\"\"\"\n   257\t        mark = len(path)\n   258\t\n   259\t        free = free_moves(closed, opened)\n   260\t        if free:\n   261\t            bits = free\n   262\t            while bits:\n   263\t                bit = bits & -bits\n   264\t                bits ^= bit\n   265\t                path.append(bit.bit_length() - 1)\n   266\t            closed |= free\n   267\t\n   268\t        if closed == full:\n   269\t            return True\n   270\t        if memo and closed in refuted:\n   271\t            del path[mark:]\n   272\t            return False\n   273\t\n   274\t        remaining = full & ~closed\n   275\t        seen &= remaining\n   276\t        candidates = remaining & ~seen if old_move else remaining\n   277\t        if restrict:\n   278\t            narrowed = remaining & opened\n   279\t            # An empty frontier means a disconnected remainder, where some\n   280\t            # stack has to be opened before anything can close.\n   281\t            if narrowed:\n   282\t                candidates = narrowed\n   283\t\n   284\t        # o(c,S) = N(c) - O(S), the stacks closing c would newly open.\n   285\t        opens: dict[int, int] = {}\n   286\t        bits = remaining\n   287\t        while bits:\n   288\t            bit = bits & -bits\n   289\t            bits ^= bit\n   290\t            customer = bit.bit_length() - 1\n   291\t            opens[customer] = masks[customer] & ~opened\n   292\t\n   293\t        # Expansion cut. Closing any `t` of the remaining customers costs at\n   294\t        # least `open_now + m_(t) - t + 1`, where `m_(t)` is the t-th smallest\n   295\t        # number of stacks a single remaining customer would newly open: any\n   296\t        # `t` distinct customers include one whose own count is at least that.\n   297\t        # `t = 1` is the cost cut below; `t >= 2` is what this adds. The value\n   298\t        # depends only on `closed` -- `opened` is a function of it -- so a state\n   299\t        # refuted here is refuted by whatever path reached it, and the memo\n   300\t        # stays sound.\n   301\t        if expansion_prune and opens:\n   302\t            open_now = (opened & ~closed).bit_count()\n   303\t            sizes = sorted(mask.bit_count() for mask in opens.values())\n   304\t            floor = 0\n   305\t            for step, size in enumerate(sizes, start=1):\n   306\t                need = size - step + 1\n   307\t                if need > floor:\n   308\t                    floor = need\n   309\t            if open_now + floor > k:\n   310\t                if memo and len(refuted) < memo_limit:\n   311\t                    refuted.add(closed)\n   312\t                del path[mark:]\n   313\t                return False\n   314\t\n   315\t        playable: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n   316\t        bits = candidates\n   317\t        while bits:\n   318\t            bit = bits & -bits\n   319\t            bits ^= bit\n   320\t            customer = bit.bit_length() - 1\n   321\t            cost = ((opened | masks[customer]) & ~closed).bit_count()\n   322\t            if cost <= k:\n   323\t                playable.append((cost, customer))\n   324\t\n   325\t        if playable and (subset_rule or definite_move):\n   326\t            playable = _apply_dominance(\n   327\t                playable, opens, subset_rule, definite_move)\n   328\t\n   329\t        if fan_order == \"index\":\n   330\t            playable.sort()\n   331\t        else:\n   332\t            playable.sort(key=fan_sort_key(fan_order, masks, remaining))\n   333\t        if branch is not None and len(playable) > 1:\n   334\t            playable = branch(closed, opened, playable)\n   335\t        for _, customer in playable:\n   336\t            state[\"nodes\"] += 1\n   337\t            if max_nodes is not None and state[\"nodes\"] > max_nodes:\n   338\t                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n   339\t                del path[mark:]\n   340\t                return False\n   341\t            if (deadline is not None and state[\"nodes\"] % 4096 == 0\n   342\t                    and time.monotonic() > deadline):\n   343\t                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n   344\t                del path[mark:]\n   345\t                return False\n   346\t\n   347\t            here = len(path)\n   348\t            path.append(customer)\n   349\t            inherited = 0\n   350\t            if old_move and seen:\n   351\t                # Q(S ++ [c]) keeps those q whose reinsertion still leaves this\n   352\t                # last move playable; everything earlier is playable already by\n   353\t                # q being in Q(S). Auto-closures can only lower that cost, so\n   354\t                # checking the move alone is conservative in the safe direction.\n   355\t                bits = seen\n   356\t                while bits:\n   357\t                    bit = bits & -bits\n   358\t                    bits ^= bit\n   359\t                    other = bit.bit_length() - 1\n   360\t                    cost = ((opened | masks[other] | masks[customer])\n   361\t                            & ~(closed | bit)).bit_count()\n   362\t                    if cost <= k:\n   363\t                        inherited |= bit\n   364\t            if search(closed | (1 << customer), opened | masks[customer],\n   365\t                      inherited):\n   366\t                return True\n   367\t            del path[here:]\n   368\t            if state[\"aborted\"]:\n   369\t                del path[mark:]\n   370\t                return False\n   371\t            # This branch is now searched, so a later sibling that could play it\n   372\t            # instead would be repeating it.\n   373\t            seen |= 1 << customer\n   374\t\n   375\t        # Only a genuine exhaustion may be recorded: a budget abort has not\n   376\t        # refuted anything, and memoising it would turn a timeout into a\n   377\t        # permanent wrong answer.\n   378\t        if memo and not state[\"aborted\"] and len(refuted) < memo_limit:\n   379\t            refuted.add(closed)\n   380\t        del path[mark:]\n   381\t        return False\n   382\t\n   383\t    found = search(0, 0)\n   384\t    if found:\n   385\t        return Decision(\"sat\", list(path), state[\"nodes\"])\n   386\t    if state[\"aborted\"] or restrict:\n   387\t        return Decision(\"unknown\", None, state[\"nodes\"])\n   388\t    return Decision(\"unsat\", None, state[\"nodes\"])\n   389\t\n   390\t\n   391\tdef _apply_dominance(\n   392\t    playable: list[tuple[int, int]],\n   393\t    opens: dict[int, int],\n   394\t    subset_rule: bool,\n   395\t    definite_move: bool,\n   396\t) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n   397\t    \"\"\"Cut the candidate list by the two dominance relations.\n   398\t\n   399\t    `close(q,S) = |{d ∉ S : o(d,S) ⊆ o(q,S)}|` counts the stacks that closing\n   400\t    `q` releases: `d` is finished once every stack it touches has been opened,\n   401\t    which after `q` means `o(d,S) ⊆ o(q,S)`. Both `close` and the subset test\n   402\t    fall out of the same pass over the remaining customers.\n   403\t    \"\"\"\n   404\t    if definite_move:\n   405\t        for cost, q in playable:\n   406\t            own = opens[q]\n   407\t            opened_by_q = own.bit_count()\n   408\t            closed_by_q = sum(1 for other in opens.values() if other & ~own == 0)\n   409\t            if closed_by_q >= opened_by_q:\n   410\t                # q is at least as good as anything else here, so every other\n   411\t                # branch can go.\n   412\t                return [(cost, q)]\n   413\t\n   414\t    if not subset_rule:\n   415\t        return playable\n   416\t\n   417\t    kept = []\n   418\t    for cost, q in playable:\n   419\t        own = opens[q]\n   420\t        dominated = any(\n   421\t            other & ~own == 0 and (other != own or d < q)\n   422\t            for d, other in opens.items() if d != q\n   423\t        )\n   424\t        if not dominated:\n   425\t            kept.append((cost, q))\n   426\t    # Every candidate dominated by a non-candidate (possible only under the\n   427\t    # frontier restriction) would empty the list; keep the original in that case.\n   428\t    return kept or playable\n   429\t\n   430\t\n   431\t@dataclass\n   432\tclass Solution:\n   433\t    \"\"\"What a descent established, and how -- which is not the same question.\n   434\t\n   435\t    `proof` is \"refutation\" when the search exhausted `value - 1`, \"bound\" when\n   436\t    `value` met a lower bound that needed no refutation, and empty when the\n   437\t    descent ran out of budget. The last is still an upper bound worth keeping\n   438\t    but is not an optimality claim, which is the distinction the `provenance`\n   439\t    field in `solutions/` exists to preserve.\n   440\t\n   441\t    `order` is empty when `solve` was given an `upper` it never improved on; the\n   442\t    caller keeps whatever witness it already had in that case.\n   443\t    \"\"\"\n   444\t\n   445\t    value: int\n   446\t    order: list[int]\n   447\t    proof: str\n   448\t    nodes: int\n   449\t    seconds: float\n   450\t\n   451\t    @property\n   452\t    def proved(self) -> bool:\n   453\t        return bool(self.proof)\n   454\t\n   455\t\n   456\tdef solve(\n   457\t    instance: MOSPInstance,\n   458\t    *,\n   459\t    upper: int | None = None,\n   460\t    upper_strategy: str = \"rule+cs-dfs\",\n   461\t    lower: int = 0,\n   462\t    max_nodes: int | None = None,\n   463\t    time_budget: float | None = None,\n   464\t    on_improve: \"Callable[[int, list[int]], None] | None\" = None,\n   465\t    **kwargs: object,\n   466\t) -> Solution:\n   467\t    \"\"\"Descend `k` with the complete search until it refutes or runs out.\n   468\t\n   469\t    Each satisfiable answer is re-simulated on the product order it induces\n   470\t    rather than trusted at `k`: the search's own measure can over-charge an\n   471\t    order no product sequence realises, so the witness is often worth more than\n   472\t    the `k` it was found at, and the next step starts from what it actually\n   473\t    achieves. That is also the point at which a wrong witness would be caught.\n   474\t\n   475\t    Args:\n   476\t        upper: where to start. Defaults to the `cs-dfs` heuristic bound.\n   477\t        lower: a known lower bound. Reaching it ends the descent with a proof\n   478\t            and no refutation, since nothing below it can be satisfiable.\n   479\t        max_nodes, time_budget: per-`k` budgets. An exhausted budget stops the\n   480\t            descent with `proved` false.\n   481\t        upper_strategy: the named strategy in `satisfiability.heuristics` that\n   482\t            supplies the starting upper bound when `upper` is not given. Every\n   483\t            stack it saves is a whole `decide` call the descent never makes, and\n   484\t            the calls it skips are the cheap ones at the top -- but it also\n   485\t            starts the expensive refutation nearer the optimum. `learned+cs-dfs`\n   486\t            starts lower than the `cs-dfs` default on 709 of the 6,376 corpus\n   487\t            instances (`reports/learning.md` §2.1).\n   488\t        on_improve: called with `(value, closing order)` each time the descent\n   489\t            lowers its bound. Without it a long run holds everything it has\n   490\t            found in memory until it returns, so an interrupted descent loses\n   491\t            all of it -- on a multi-day budget that is the difference between a\n   492\t            crash costing minutes and costing days.\n   493\t\n   494\t    Returns:\n   495\t        A `Solution` whose `order` is a customer closing order; pass it through\n   496\t        `product_order_from_customers` for a production sequence.\n   497\t    \"\"\"\n   498\t    from satisfiability.heuristics import upper_bound\n   499\t\n   500\t    started = time.monotonic()\n   501\t    nodes = 0\n   502\t\n   503\t    if upper is None:\n   504\t        value, ordering = upper_bound(instance, upper_strategy)\n   505\t        order = _closing_order(instance, ordering)\n   506\t    else:\n   507\t        value = upper\n   508\t        order = []\n   509\t\n   510\t    if value <= lower:\n   511\t        return Solution(value, order, \"bound\", 0, time.monotonic() - started)\n   512\t\n   513\t    k = value - 1\n   514\t    while k >= lower:\n   515\t        deadline = None if time_budget is None else started + time_budget\n   516\t        answer = decide(instance, k, max_nodes=max_nodes, deadline=deadline, **kwargs)\n   517\t        nodes += answer.nodes\n   518\t\n   519\t        if answer.status == \"unsat\":\n   520\t            # The refutation says the optimum is above k. Claiming it *is*\n   521\t            # k + 1 needs a witness that achieves k + 1, and the witness in\n   522\t            # hand is only trusted at the value it simulates to. The search\n   523\t            # scores closing orders, not the product sequences they build, and\n   524\t            # the two agree on every instance measured -- but a proof that\n   525\t            # rests on \"measured\" rather than \"checked here\" is the kind this\n   526\t            # project has had to retract before.\n   527\t            if value == k + 1:\n   528\t                return Solution(value, order, \"refutation\", nodes,\n   529\t                                time.monotonic() - started)\n   530\t            return Solution(value, order, \"\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n   531\t        if answer.status == \"unknown\":\n   532\t            return Solution(value, order, \"\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n   533\t\n   534\t        found = answer.order\n   535\t        achieved = max_open_stacks(\n   536\t            instance, product_order_from_customers(instance, found))\n   537\t        if achieved < value:\n   538\t            value, order = achieved, found\n   539\t            if on_improve is not None:\n   540\t                on_improve(value, order)\n   541\t        k = min(k, achieved) - 1\n   542\t\n   543\t    # The descent walked down to the lower bound: nothing below it can be\n   544\t    # satisfiable, so no refutation is needed.\n   545\t    return Solution(value, order, \"bound\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n   546\t\n   547\t\n   548\tdef _closing_order(instance: MOSPInstance, ordering: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n   549\t    \"\"\"The closing order a product sequence induces, customers by last product.\"\"\"\n   550\t    position = {pattern: i for i, pattern in enumerate(ordering)}\n   551\t    active = [c for c in range(instance.n_customers) if instance.customer_patterns(c)]\n   552\t    return sorted(\n   553\t        active,\n   554\t        key=lambda c: (max(position[p] for p in instance.customer_patterns(c)), c),\n   555\t    )","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ca0f07da-33a4-48f2-8c42-70a27d941600","uuid":"21db73f2-b46a-479b-9de5-33f0b53e7c04","timestamp":"2026-09-30T21:30:53.603Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     1\t\"\"\"Complete search over customer closing orders (Chu & Stuckey 2009, §2-3).\n     2\t\n     3\tAn alternative to the SAT encoding for deciding \"MOSP(I) <= k?\". It searches the\n     4\tspace the plan's §0.1 established is the right one -- orders in which customers'\n     5\tstacks *close* -- rather than the space of product sequences, and prunes it with\n     6\tthe dominance relations of their §3, none of which can be written as clauses\n     7\tbecause every one conditions on the partial sequence already committed.\n     8\t\n     9\tWhy this exists alongside the SAT path: their Table 1 reports the complete\n    10\tsearch closing 125-125-6, 125-125-8 and 125-125-10 in 9 s, 0.19 s and 0.02 s,\n    11\tagainst our SAT refutations, which do not return on those instances at all.\n    12\tThe densities where their search is fastest are the ones holding most of our\n    13\topen gap. It is also the reverse of the SAT path's profile -- their search is\n    14\tslowest exactly where sparsity makes the branching factor explode, which is what\n    15\ttheir relaxation technique (plan item 5) exists to repair.\n    16\t\n    17\t**The measure.** A state is the set `S` of customers already closed. `O(S)` is\n    18\tthe set opened so far, the union of the neighbourhoods `N(c)` over `c ∈ S`, with\n    19\t`N` self-inclusive. Closing `c` next costs `|O(S ∪ {c}) - S|`: the stacks opened\n    20\tbut not yet closed, counting `c` itself, which is still open at the moment it\n    21\tcloses. `min` over closing orders of the peak of this equals the true optimum --\n    22\tmeasured 400/400 against exhaustive search, `reports/chu_stuckey_plan.md` §0.1.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Free moves.** A remaining customer whose whole neighbourhood is already opened\n    25\tcosts nothing to close and is closed immediately. This is the `open = 0` case of\n    26\ttheir Theorem 1 and it is what keeps the measure above honest: without it, a\n    27\tcustomer whose products are all made would keep counting as an open stack, and\n    28\tthe \"close\" counts the dominance rules below are stated in terms of would be\n    29\twrong.\n    30\t\n    31\t**The rules.** Each is a dominance: it discards branches that cannot beat the\n    32\tbranches kept, so a refutation still refutes.\n    33\t\n    34\t- *subset* (their §2): if `o(cᵢ,S) ⊆ o(cⱼ,S)` then closing `cᵢ` first is never\n    35\t  worse, since closing `cⱼ` opens everything `cᵢ` would have and closing `cᵢ`\n    36\t  can only help `cⱼ` -- so `cⱼ` leaves the candidate set.\n    37\t- *definite move* (their Theorem 1): if `close(q,S) ≥ open(q,S)` and `S ++ [q]`\n    38\t  is playable, then every solution extending `S` has one extending `S ++ [q]`,\n    39\t  so **all** other branches go.\n    40\t- *old move* (their Theorem 3): if `q` was already searched at an ancestor and\n    41\t  inserting `q` back there leaves the sequence playable, that subtree has been\n    42\t  seen and `q` goes. Maintained as a set `Q(S)` in `O(|C|)` per node.\n    43\t- *better move* (their Theorem 2): if `S ++ [q]` and `S ++ [r, q]` are playable\n    44\t  and `close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r})`, then `r` goes. `O(|R|³)` per node\n    45\t  against Theorem 1's `O(|R|²)`, implemented in the C only, and **worth it only\n    46\t  on sparse instances** -- see `sparse_enough_for_better_move`.\n    47\t\n    48\t**Old move and the memo, together.** Chu & Stuckey run both -- \"better move\",\n    49\t\"old move\" and nogood recording on at once -- and the C follows them. The\n    50\tPython still refuses the combination, because the argument against it has not\n    51\tbeen retired: a failure reached with old-move pruning depends on which branches\n    52\tan *ancestor* had searched, so it is a property of the path rather than of `S`,\n    53\tand recording it against `S` alone could refute a state that some other path\n    54\twould not. Whether that can actually happen is settled by the exhaustive tests\n    55\tin `tests/test_native.py`, not by the argument.\n    56\t\"\"\"\n    57\t\n    58\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n    59\t\n    60\timport time\n    61\tfrom collections.abc import Callable\n    62\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n    63\t\n    64\tfrom mosp.instance import MOSPInstance\n    65\tfrom mosp.verify import max_open_stacks\n    66\tfrom satisfiability.heuristics import (\n    67\t    FAN_ORDERS,\n    68\t    _neighbour_masks,\n    69\t    fan_sort_key,\n    70\t    product_order_from_customers,\n    71\t)\n    72\t\n    73\t\n    74\t@dataclass\n    75\tclass Decision:\n    76\t    \"\"\"The answer to \"MOSP(I) <= k?\", and what it cost to get it.\n    77\t\n    78\t    `status` is \"sat\" with a closing order, \"unsat\" -- a genuine refutation, the\n    79\t    whole space having been searched -- or \"unknown\", which means the node\n    80\t    budget ran out and nothing was proved either way. A restricted search never\n    81\t    returns \"unsat\": the restriction discards branches it cannot justify, so\n    82\t    exhausting what remains proves nothing.\n    83\t    \"\"\"\n    84\t\n    85\t    status: str\n    86\t    order: list[int] | None\n    87\t    nodes: int\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\t# Products per customer below which Theorem 2 pays. Measured on refutations\n    91\t# whose answers were already known, counting branch decisions, which is the one\n    92\t# figure a loaded machine cannot distort:\n    93\t#\n    94\t#   products/customer   nodes without   with Theorem 2   verdict\n    95\t#   2.6 (open instance)   263,000,000+        885,759    closed it, in 3s\n    96\t#   2.3                     7,327,112         55,424     15x faster\n    97\t#   6.1                    13,300,731      7,825,793     0.8x, a slight loss\n    98\t#   8.0                    11,902,214      8,652,247     0.45x, a clear loss\n    99\t#\n   100\t# The rule inverts with density because the theorem ranges over pairs of\n   101\t# candidates: sparse instances keep many candidates playable at each step, which\n   102\t# is exactly where pruning a pair at a time repays its cubic cost. Dense ones\n   103\t# run out of candidates quickly and the cost is wasted.\n   104\tBETTER_MOVE_DENSITY = 5.0\n   105\t\n   106\t\n   107\tdef sparse_enough_for_better_move(instance: MOSPInstance) -> bool:\n   108\t    \"\"\"Whether Theorem 2 should be used on this instance.\n   109\t\n   110\t    Chosen by measurement rather than by the instance's name: the Chu & Stuckey\n   111\t    generator's density parameter is not recorded in the file, and the ratio\n   112\t    below reproduces it closely enough (2.57 for a \"-2-\" instance, 8.01 for\n   113\t    a \"-8-\").\n   114\t    \"\"\"\n   115\t    if instance.n_customers == 0:\n   116\t        return False\n   117\t    return float(instance.matrix.sum()) / instance.n_customers <= BETTER_MOVE_DENSITY\n   118\t\n   119\t\n   120\tdef decide(\n   121\t    instance: MOSPInstance,\n   122\t    k: int,\n   123\t    *,\n   124\t    restrict: bool = False,\n   125\t    subset_rule: bool = True,\n   126\t    definite_move: bool = True,\n   127\t    old_move: bool = True,\n   128\t    memo: bool = True,\n   129\t    max_nodes: int | None = None,\n   130\t    deadline: float | None = None,\n   131\t    memo_limit: int = 4_000_000,\n   132\t    native: bool = True,\n   133\t    branch: \"Callable[[int, int, list[tuple[int, int]]], list[tuple[int, int]]] | None\" = None,\n   134\t    expansion_prune: bool = False,\n   135\t    fan_order: str = \"index\",\n   136\t    **kwargs: object,\n   137\t) -> Decision:\n   138\t    \"\"\"Decide \"MOSP(instance) <= k?\" by searching customer closing orders.\n   139\t\n   140\t    Args:\n   141\t        k: the budget on simultaneously open stacks.\n   142\t        restrict: branch only on `R ∩ O(S)`, customers already open. This is\n   143\t            their `ub_MOSP` (§3.4) and is **not sound** -- it answers \"sat\" or\n   144\t            \"unknown\", never \"unsat\".\n   145\t        subset_rule, definite_move, old_move: the dominance relations above. The\n   146\t            flags exist so the exhaustive tests can vary one at a time.\n   147\t            `old_move` cannot be combined with `memo`, for the reason above.\n   148\t        memo: record states already refuted, their `prob[S]`. The state is\n   149\t            `S` alone -- `O(S)` is a function of it -- so a failure at `S` is a\n   150\t            failure by whatever path reached it.\n   151\t        max_nodes: abort after this many branches and return \"unknown\".\n   152\t        deadline: `time.monotonic()` value to stop at, checked every 4,096\n   153\t            nodes. Same effect as `max_nodes`, in the unit a caller with a\n   154\t            budget actually has.\n   155\t        memo_limit: stop growing the memo past this many states. The table is\n   156\t            what bounds memory here, and an unbounded one on a 125-customer\n   157\t            refutation will take the machine down.\n   158\t        native: use the C port when it applies. It visits the same nodes in the\n   159\t            same order -- the counts match exactly on every instance measured --\n   160\t            about 120x faster. Set False to force the Python, which is the\n   161\t            reference implementation and what the exhaustive tests are written\n   162\t            against. `satisfiability/native.py` says when the C declines.\n   163\t        expansion_prune: cut a whole node when even the best continuation must\n   164\t            exceed `k`, by the neighbourhood-expansion argument applied to the\n   165\t            customers still remaining rather than to the instance as a whole\n   166\t            (`reports/expansion_bound.md`). The rule generalises the cost cut\n   167\t            this search already makes: that cut is the one-step case, and every\n   168\t            further step is new pruning. Costs one pass over data the node has\n   169\t            already computed. **Forces the Python path** -- the C does not\n   170\t            implement it -- so it is for measuring whether a port is worth\n   171\t            writing.\n   172\t        branch: reorder the surviving candidates at each node. Called with\n   173\t            `(closed, opened, playable)` where `playable` is the `(cost,\n   174\t            customer)` list the dominance rules left, and must return a\n   175\t            permutation of it -- dropping a candidate would make a refutation\n   176\t            unsound, and the caller is trusted not to. Default is cheapest\n   177\t            first. **Forces the Python path**: the C has no way to call back,\n   178\t            so this is for measuring whether a different order is worth porting,\n   179\t            not for production. Order affects only *which* branches are visited\n   180\t            first: the cost cut, the memo and every dominance rule are\n   181\t            properties of the state, not of the order they are reached in.\n   182\t        fan_order: how equal-cost candidates are ordered. `\"index\"` (the\n   183\t            default, and what every recorded node count was made with) breaks\n   184\t            ties by customer index; `\"degree\"` puts the candidate with the\n   185\t            most neighbours not yet closed first, then index -- the fan order\n   186\t            of the two-key rule in `reports/ml_nature.md` §7. Implemented in\n   187\t            both the C and the Python, so it costs nothing to ask for. Like\n   188\t            `branch`, it changes which branches are visited first and never\n   189\t            which are visited, so the answer is the same under either; the\n   190\t            node count is what it is measured on (plan 2 §2.5a).\n   191\t\n   192\t    Returns:\n   193\t        A `Decision`. The \"sat\" order closes every customer with a non-empty\n   194\t        product set; customers needing nothing are omitted, as they never open.\n   195\t    \"\"\"\n   196\t    if fan_order not in FAN_ORDERS:\n   197\t        raise ValueError(f\"fan_order must be one of {FAN_ORDERS}, not {fan_order!r}\")\n   198\t\n   199\t    if native and branch is None and not expansion_prune:\n   200\t        from satisfiability.native import decide_native\n   201\t        answer = decide_native(\n   202\t            instance, k, restrict=restrict, subset_rule=subset_rule,\n   203\t            definite_move=definite_move, old_move=old_move, memo=memo,\n   204\t            better_move=kwargs.pop(\"better_move\", False),\n   205\t            better_move_dominators=kwargs.pop(\"better_move_dominators\", 4),\n   206\t            # Reverts of the 2026-09-26 fix, unsound, for measurement only\n   207\t            # (learning.fix_cost); meaningless without `better_move`.\n   208\t            old_close_count=bool(kwargs.pop(\"old_close_count\", False)),\n   209\t            old_rule_order=bool(kwargs.pop(\"old_rule_order\", False)),\n   210\t            subset_after_better_move=bool(kwargs.pop(\"subset_after_better_move\", False)),\n   211\t            max_nodes=max_nodes, memo_limit=memo_limit,\n   212\t            seconds=None if deadline is None else max(0.0, deadline - time.monotonic()),\n   213\t            fan_order=fan_order)\n   214\t        if answer is not None:\n   215\t            return answer\n   216\t\n   217\t    if old_move and memo:\n   218\t        # The C runs both, as Chu & Stuckey do, and 10,476 exhaustive decisions\n   219\t        # agree with brute force. This Python reference keeps the stricter\n   220\t        # reading -- the memo is dropped rather than combined -- so that the two\n   221\t        # implementations are not both resting on the same assumption.\n   222\t        memo = False\n   223\t\n   224\t    masks = _neighbour_masks(instance)\n   225\t    active = [c for c in range(instance.n_customers) if instance.customer_patterns(c)]\n   226\t\n   227\t    full = 0\n   228\t    for customer in active:\n   229\t        full |= 1 << customer\n   230\t\n   231\t    if not active:\n   232\t        return Decision(\"sat\", [], 0)\n   233\t\n   234\t    path: list[int] = []\n   235\t    refuted: set[int] = set()\n   236\t    state = {\"nodes\": 0, \"aborted\": False}\n   237\t\n   238\t    def free_moves(closed: int, opened: int) -> int:\n   239\t        \"\"\"Customers whose neighbourhood is wholly opened: free to close now.\n   240\t\n   241\t        Closing one opens nothing, so the peak cannot rise, and it can only\n   242\t        release later steps. Closing them does not change `O`, so no further\n   243\t        free moves appear and one pass suffices.\n   244\t        \"\"\"\n   245\t        found = 0\n   246\t        bits = full & ~closed\n   247\t        while bits:\n   248\t            bit = bits & -bits\n   249\t            bits ^= bit\n   250\t            if masks[bit.bit_length() - 1] & ~opened == 0:\n   251\t                found |= bit\n   252\t        return found\n   253\t\n   254\t    def search(closed: int, opened: int, seen: int = 0) -> bool:\n   255\t        \"\"\"`seen` is Q(S): moves an ancestor already searched that could be\n   256\t        played here instead, without making the sequence unplayable.\"\"\"\n   257\t        mark = len(path)\n   258\t\n   259\t        free = free_moves(closed, opened)\n   260\t        if free:\n   261\t            bits = free\n   262\t            while bits:\n   263\t                bit = bits & -bits\n   264\t                bits ^= bit\n   265\t                path.append(bit.bit_length() - 1)\n   266\t            closed |= free\n   267\t\n   268\t        if closed == full:\n   269\t            return True\n   270\t        if memo and closed in refuted:\n   271\t            del path[mark:]\n   272\t            return False\n   273\t\n   274\t        remaining = full & ~closed\n   275\t        seen &= remaining\n   276\t        candidates = remaining & ~seen if old_move else remaining\n   277\t        if restrict:\n   278\t            narrowed = remaining & opened\n   279\t            # An empty frontier means a disconnected remainder, where some\n   280\t            # stack has to be opened before anything can close.\n   281\t            if narrowed:\n   282\t                candidates = narrowed\n   283\t\n   284\t        # o(c,S) = N(c) - O(S), the stacks closing c would newly open.\n   285\t        opens: dict[int, int] = {}\n   286\t        bits = remaining\n   287\t        while bits:\n   288\t            bit = bits & -bits\n   289\t            bits ^= bit\n   290\t            customer = bit.bit_length() - 1\n   291\t            opens[customer] = masks[customer] & ~opened\n   292\t\n   293\t        # Expansion cut. Closing any `t` of the remaining customers costs at\n   294\t        # least `open_now + m_(t) - t + 1`, where `m_(t)` is the t-th smallest\n   295\t        # number of stacks a single remaining customer would newly open: any\n   296\t        # `t` distinct customers include one whose own count is at least that.\n   297\t        # `t = 1` is the cost cut below; `t >= 2` is what this adds. The value\n   298\t        # depends only on `closed` -- `opened` is a function of it -- so a state\n   299\t        # refuted here is refuted by whatever path reached it, and the memo\n   300\t        # stays sound.\n   301\t        if expansion_prune and opens:\n   302\t            open_now = (opened & ~closed).bit_count()\n   303\t            sizes = sorted(mask.bit_count() for mask in opens.values())\n   304\t            floor = 0\n   305\t            for step, size in enumerate(sizes, start=1):\n   306\t                need = size - step + 1\n   307\t                if need > floor:\n   308\t                    floor = need\n   309\t            if open_now + floor > k:\n   310\t                if memo and len(refuted) < memo_limit:\n   311\t                    refuted.add(closed)\n   312\t                del path[mark:]\n   313\t                return False\n   314\t\n   315\t        playable: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n   316\t        bits = candidates\n   317\t        while bits:\n   318\t            bit = bits & -bits\n   319\t            bits ^= bit\n   320\t            customer = bit.bit_length() - 1\n   321\t            cost = ((opened | masks[customer]) & ~closed).bit_count()\n   322\t            if cost <= k:\n   323\t                playable.append((cost, customer))\n   324\t\n   325\t        if playable and (subset_rule or definite_move):\n   326\t            playable = _apply_dominance(\n   327\t                playable, opens, subset_rule, definite_move)\n   328\t\n   329\t        if fan_order == \"index\":\n   330\t            playable.sort()\n   331\t        else:\n   332\t            playable.sort(key=fan_sort_key(fan_order, masks, remaining))\n   333\t        if branch is not None and len(playable) > 1:\n   334\t            playable = branch(closed, opened, playable)\n   335\t        for _, customer in playable:\n   336\t            state[\"nodes\"] += 1\n   337\t            if max_nodes is not None and state[\"nodes\"] > max_nodes:\n   338\t                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n   339\t                del path[mark:]\n   340\t                return False\n   341\t            if (deadline is not None and state[\"nodes\"] % 4096 == 0\n   342\t                    and time.monotonic() > deadline):\n   343\t                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n   344\t                del path[mark:]\n   345\t                return False\n   346\t\n   347\t            here = len(path)\n   348\t            path.append(customer)\n   349\t            inherited = 0\n   350\t            if old_move and seen:\n   351\t                # Q(S ++ [c]) keeps those q whose reinsertion still leaves this\n   352\t                # last move playable; everything earlier is playable already by\n   353\t                # q being in Q(S). Auto-closures can only lower that cost, so\n   354\t                # checking the move alone is conservative in the safe direction.\n   355\t                bits = seen\n   356\t                while bits:\n   357\t                    bit = bits & -bits\n   358\t                    bits ^= bit\n   359\t                    other = bit.bit_length() - 1\n   360\t                    cost = ((opened | masks[other] | masks[customer])\n   361\t                            & ~(closed | bit)).bit_count()\n   362\t                    if cost <= k:\n   363\t                        inherited |= bit\n   364\t            if search(closed | (1 << customer), opened | masks[customer],\n   365\t                      inherited):\n   366\t                return True\n   367\t            del path[here:]\n   368\t            if state[\"aborted\"]:\n   369\t                del path[mark:]\n   370\t                return False\n   371\t            # This branch is now searched, so a later sibling that could play it\n   372\t            # instead would be repeating it.\n   373\t            seen |= 1 << customer\n   374\t\n   375\t        # Only a genuine exhaustion may be recorded: a budget abort has not\n   376\t        # refuted anything, and memoising it would turn a timeout into a\n   377\t        # permanent wrong answer.\n   378\t        if memo and not state[\"aborted\"] and len(refuted) < memo_limit:\n   379\t            refuted.add(closed)\n   380\t        del path[mark:]\n   381\t        return False\n   382\t\n   383\t    found = search(0, 0)\n   384\t    if found:\n   385\t        return Decision(\"sat\", list(path), state[\"nodes\"])\n   386\t    if state[\"aborted\"] or restrict:\n   387\t        return Decision(\"unknown\", None, state[\"nodes\"])\n   388\t    return Decision(\"unsat\", None, state[\"nodes\"])\n   389\t\n   390\t\n   391\tdef _apply_dominance(\n   392\t    playable: list[tuple[int, int]],\n   393\t    opens: dict[int, int],\n   394\t    subset_rule: bool,\n   395\t    definite_move: bool,\n   396\t) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n   397\t    \"\"\"Cut the candidate list by the two dominance relations.\n   398\t\n   399\t    `close(q,S) = |{d ∉ S : o(d,S) ⊆ o(q,S)}|` counts the stacks that closing\n   400\t    `q` releases: `d` is finished once every stack it touches has been opened,\n   401\t    which after `q` means `o(d,S) ⊆ o(q,S)`. Both `close` and the subset test\n   402\t    fall out of the same pass over the remaining customers.\n   403\t    \"\"\"\n   404\t    if definite_move:\n   405\t        for cost, q in playable:\n   406\t            own = opens[q]\n   407\t            opened_by_q = own.bit_count()\n   408\t            closed_by_q = sum(1 for other in opens.values() if other & ~own == 0)\n   409\t            if closed_by_q >= opened_by_q:\n   410\t                # q is at least as good as anything else here, so every other\n   411\t                # branch can go.\n   412\t                return [(cost, q)]\n   413\t\n   414\t    if not subset_rule:\n   415\t        return playable\n   416\t\n   417\t    kept = []\n   418\t    for cost, q in playable:\n   419\t        own = opens[q]\n   420\t        dominated = any(\n   421\t            other & ~own == 0 and (other != own or d < q)\n   422\t            for d, other in opens.items() if d != q\n   423\t        )\n   424\t        if not dominated:\n   425\t            kept.append((cost, q))\n   426\t    # Every candidate dominated by a non-candidate (possible only under the\n   427\t    # frontier restriction) would empty the list; keep the original in that case.\n   428\t    return kept or playable\n   429\t\n   430\t\n   431\t@dataclass\n   432\tclass Solution:\n   433\t    \"\"\"What a descent established, and how -- which is not the same question.\n   434\t\n   435\t    `proof` is \"refutation\" when the search exhausted `value - 1`, \"bound\" when\n   436\t    `value` met a lower bound that needed no refutation, and empty when the\n   437\t    descent ran out of budget. The last is still an upper bound worth keeping\n   438\t    but is not an optimality claim, which is the distinction the `provenance`\n   439\t    field in `solutions/` exists to preserve.\n   440\t\n   441\t    `order` is empty when `solve` was given an `upper` it never improved on; the\n   442\t    caller keeps whatever witness it already had in that case.\n   443\t    \"\"\"\n   444\t\n   445\t    value: int\n   446\t    order: list[int]\n   447\t    proof: str\n   448\t    nodes: int\n   449\t    seconds: float\n   450\t\n   451\t    @property\n   452\t    def proved(self) -> bool:\n   453\t        return bool(self.proof)\n   454\t\n   455\t\n   456\tdef solve(\n   457\t    instance: MOSPInstance,\n   458\t    *,\n   459\t    upper: int | None = None,\n   460\t    upper_strategy: str = \"rule+cs-dfs\",\n   461\t    lower: int = 0,\n   462\t    max_nodes: int | None = None,\n   463\t    time_budget: float | None = None,\n   464\t    on_improve: \"Callable[[int, list[int]], None] | None\" = None,\n   465\t    **kwargs: object,\n   466\t) -> Solution:\n   467\t    \"\"\"Descend `k` with the complete search until it refutes or runs out.\n   468\t\n   469\t    Each satisfiable answer is re-simulated on the product order it induces\n   470\t    rather than trusted at `k`: the search's own measure can over-charge an\n   471\t    order no product sequence realises, so the witness is often worth more than\n   472\t    the `k` it was found at, and the next step starts from what it actually\n   473\t    achieves. That is also the point at which a wrong witness would be caught.\n   474\t\n   475\t    Args:\n   476\t        upper: where to start. Defaults to the `cs-dfs` heuristic bound.\n   477\t        lower: a known lower bound. Reaching it ends the descent with a proof\n   478\t            and no refutation, since nothing below it can be satisfiable.\n   479\t        max_nodes, time_budget: per-`k` budgets. An exhausted budget stops the\n   480\t            descent with `proved` false.\n   481\t        upper_strategy: the named strategy in `satisfiability.heuristics` that\n   482\t            supplies the starting upper bound when `upper` is not given. Every\n   483\t            stack it saves is a whole `decide` call the descent never makes, and\n   484\t            the calls it skips are the cheap ones at the top -- but it also\n   485\t            starts the expensive refutation nearer the optimum. `learned+cs-dfs`\n   486\t            starts lower than the `cs-dfs` default on 709 of the 6,376 corpus\n   487\t            instances (`reports/learning.md` §2.1).\n   488\t        on_improve: called with `(value, closing order)` each time the descent\n   489\t            lowers its bound. Without it a long run holds everything it has\n   490\t            found in memory until it returns, so an interrupted descent loses\n   491\t            all of it -- on a multi-day budget that is the difference between a\n   492\t            crash costing minutes and costing days.\n   493\t\n   494\t    Returns:\n   495\t        A `Solution` whose `order` is a customer closing order; pass it through\n   496\t        `product_order_from_customers` for a production sequence.\n   497\t    \"\"\"\n   498\t    from satisfiability.heuristics import upper_bound\n   499\t\n   500\t    started = time.monotonic()\n   501\t    nodes = 0\n   502\t\n   503\t    if upper is None:\n   504\t        value, ordering = upper_bound(instance, upper_strategy)\n   505\t        order = _closing_order(instance, ordering)\n   506\t    else:\n   507\t        value = upper\n   508\t        order = []\n   509\t\n   510\t    if value <= lower:\n   511\t        return Solution(value, order, \"bound\", 0, time.monotonic() - started)\n   512\t\n   513\t    k = value - 1\n   514\t    while k >= lower:\n   515\t        deadline = None if time_budget is None else started + time_budget\n   516\t        answer = decide(instance, k, max_nodes=max_nodes, deadline=deadline, **kwargs)\n   517\t        nodes += answer.nodes\n   518\t\n   519\t        if answer.status == \"unsat\":\n   520\t            # The refutation says the optimum is above k. Claiming it *is*\n   521\t            # k + 1 needs a witness that achieves k + 1, and the witness in\n   522\t            # hand is only trusted at the value it simulates to. The search\n   523\t            # scores closing orders, not the product sequences they build, and\n   524\t            # the two agree on every instance measured -- but a proof that\n   525\t            # rests on \"measured\" rather than \"checked here\" is the kind this\n   526\t            # project has had to retract before.\n   527\t            if value == k + 1:\n   528\t                return Solution(value, order, \"refutation\", nodes,\n   529\t                                time.monotonic() - started)\n   530\t            return Solution(value, order, \"\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n   531\t        if answer.status == \"unknown\":\n   532\t            return Solution(value, order, \"\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n   533\t\n   534\t        found = answer.order\n   535\t        achieved = max_open_stacks(\n   536\t            instance, product_order_from_customers(instance, found))\n   537\t        if achieved < value:\n   538\t            value, order = achieved, found\n   539\t            if on_improve is not None:\n   540\t                on_improve(value, order)\n   541\t        k = min(k, achieved) - 1\n   542\t\n   543\t    # The descent walked down to the lower bound: nothing below it can be\n   544\t    # satisfiable, so no refutation is needed.\n   545\t    return Solution(value, order, \"bound\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n   546\t\n   547\t\n   548\tdef _closing_order(instance: MOSPInstance, ordering: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n   549\t    \"\"\"The closing order a product sequence induces, customers by last product.\"\"\"\n   550\t    position = {pattern: i for i, pattern in enumerate(ordering)}\n   551\t    active = [c for c in range(instance.n_customers) if instance.customer_patterns(c)]\n   552\t    return sorted(\n   553\t        active,\n   554\t        key=lambda c: (max(position[p] for p in instance.customer_patterns(c)), c),\n   555\t    )","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
