\t                          index_of[kept] = index_of[i]; kept++; }\n   161\t    }\n   162\t    /* Every candidate dominated by a non-candidate would empty the list;\n   163\t     * keep the original in that case, as the Python does. */\n   164\t    return kept ? kept : count;\n   165\t}\n   166\t\n   167\t/* Theorem 2 over the candidates in `costs`/`who`/`index_of`. Returns the\n   168\t * surviving count. */\n   169\tstatic int better_move_pass(const search_t *s, mask_t closed, mask_t opened,\n   170\t                            int *costs, int *who, int *index_of, int count,\n   171\t                            mask_t *discarded) {\n   172\t    /* Theorem 2, \"better move\". If S ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are both playable\n   173\t     * and close(q, S u {r}) >= open(q, S u {r}), then any solution extending\n   174\t     * S ++ [r] has one extending S ++ [q], so r can go. Theorem 1 is the case\n   175\t     * where one q beats every r at once, which is why it runs first as a fast\n   176\t     * path: it is O(R^2) where this is O(R^3).\n   177\t     *\n   178\t     * Only the cheapest few q are tried as dominators. Using a subset prunes\n   179\t     * less but never wrongly -- the theorem justifies each pruning on its own,\n   180\t     * so leaving some unfound costs nodes, not correctness. */\n   181\t    if (count > 1) {\n   182\t        int limit = s->better_move_dominators > 0 &&\n   183\t                    s->better_move_dominators < count\n   184\t                    ? s->better_move_dominators : count;\n   185\t        /* Survivors are marked, not compacted in place. The dominator loop\n   186\t         * below reads `who[qi]` across the candidate list while the compaction\n   187\t         * writes survivors back into the same array: once anything is pruned,\n   188\t         * `kept < ri`, and `who[kept] = who[ri]` clobbers a slot the loop has\n   189\t         * yet to read. With `better_move_dominators = 0` the limit is `count`,\n   190\t         * so