project had measured it 400/400"," (`../reports/chu_stuckey_plan.md` §0.1). Item 07 turns it into a lemma."," "," ### 1.5 The search tree"]},{"oldStart":247,"oldLines":7,"newStart":248,"newLines":7,"lines":[" ### 2.2 The definite move (Chu & Stuckey Theorem 1)"," "," **Paper.** \"Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S), then if","-U′ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a solution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (p. 246).","+U′ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a solution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (PDF p. 6)."," "," **Premise, as the code checks it.** At state `S`:"," "]},{"oldStart":292,"oldLines":7,"newStart":293,"newLines":7,"lines":[" **Acyclic by construction.** One candidate is kept and every other one is"," covered by it directly."," ","-**Checker.** Step `[\"definite\", q]`. Cert:600–611 recomputes that `q` is","+**Checker.** Step `[\"definite\", q]`. Cert:602–616 recomputes that `q` is"," playable and that `close ≥ open` over `R(S)`, and makes `q` the cover of every"," other playable candidate. It does not check that `q` is the first in index"," order, which soundness does not need."]},{"oldStart":301,"oldLines":7,"newStart":302,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Paper.** \"if o(cᵢ, S) ⊆ o(cⱼ, S) and i < j, then clearly, we can always play"," i before j rather than playing j immediately, since closing j will close i in","-any case. Hence move j can be removed\" (p. 245).","+any case. Hence move j can be removed\" (PDF p. 5)."," "," **Premise, as the code checks it.** At state `S`, for a playable candidate"," `r ∈ P`: some `d ∈ R(S)` with `d ≠ r` satisfies"]},{"oldStart":313,"oldLines":7,"newStart":314,"newLines":7,"lines":[" unplayable customer, because `o(d) ⊆ o(r)` gives `cost(S, d) ≤ cost(S, r) ≤ k`."," "," **Conclusion.** `Sol_k(S·r) ⇒ Sol_k(S·d)`. Closing `r` makes `d` free","-(`o(d, S·r)`'s neighbourhood is inside `O(S ∪ {r})`), and `d` then `r` reaches","+(`N[d] ⊆ O(S) ∪ o(r, S) = O(S ∪ {r})`), and `d` then `r` reaches"," the same state as `r` alone,