{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfaMLeuQ3KLCXwbRFALVm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 05 is done: `paper2/search_soundness.md` now has §1–2, and the gate passes (`lake build` ok, `sorry` 1 of limit 1, 1,275 tests passed, 2 skipped, 1 xfailed). The item is marked `[x]`, PROGRESS.md reads 5/13, and nothing was committed.\n\n**What the document says**\n- **§1, the model:**\n  - States are closed sets with the free moves already closed, `cl(T) = fin(O(T))`. Since `O(cl T) = O(T)`, the opened set depends only on the closed set, which is why the memo can key on it.\n  - Closing `c` costs `|O(T∪{c}) ∖ T|`, and the question is `Sol_k(∅)`: can every customer be closed in some order at cost ≤ k?\n  - Lemma F says closing the free moves loses nothing.\n  - A full order's cost is `outNarrowness` in `Narrowness.lean`, so the least cost is pw + 1, which is MOSP. Proving this link in Lean is item 07's job.\n- **§2, the rules:** the free move, definite move, subset rule, corrected better move, old move and memo. Each gets its exact premise and conclusion as coded, the quoted C and Python lines, its place in the order, why it cannot cycle, and what the certificate checker verifies for it.\n  - Node soundness is stated as one condition: every chain of coverings from a discarded candidate must end at a kept move or an old move. That is the checker's step 5.\n  - Both known bad forms are located: the uncounted-free-customers close count and the cross-rule cycle.\n- **§2.9, the claim:** the full composition in the code's order answers `unsat` only if `¬Sol_k(∅)`. The five configurations that produced the corpus's refutations are all instances of it.\n\n**Findings, stated but not proved**\n1. **Every production refutation ran old move and the memo together.** `decide()` defaults to both and sends them to the C, and that is the path of `csearch`, `recertify` and the harness default. The Python reference refuses this combination and the certificate checker won't check it. §2.7 argues it is sound: a node's old moves rest only on subtrees that have already been fully refuted, so those refutations are genuine. It is recorded as a claim for item 11 to prove or refute.\n2. The C comment at `customer_search.c:178` says only \"the cheapest few q\" are tried. In fact they are the first few *by index*, because the cost sort happens after the filter. This does not affect soundness, and I left the code unchanged.\n3. Chu & Stuckey's \"synergy\" between old move and better move is implemented nowhere.\n\nBefore writing, I checked each rule's stated conclusion with a throwaway script on 1,461 `(graph, k)` pairs (graphs up to 8 vertices), with zero failures. That check did not simulate old move or the memo, since both depend on the search path; item 06 covers them. A new test, `tests/test_search_soundness_doc.py`, fails if any of the 54 quoted code lines drifts from the source, and fails if the C's `subset_rule → better_move` order changes."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":916,"cache_read_input_tokens":176782,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":916,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":745,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ca0f07da-33a4-48f2-8c42-70a27d941600","uuid":"afcf0b22-0b38-4a4a-9a3a-29be90bad955","timestamp":"2026-09-30T21:41:55.226Z","request_id":"req_011CfaMLeTcP8EqNR6jD69PJ"}
